 section has 40 questions and lasts 1 hour and 20 minutes. This section represents 50% of your total score. Your score on the multiple-choice section is based on the number of questions answered correctly. Points are not deducted for incorrect answers or unanswered questions.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003ESection II: Free Response\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp class=\\u0022p1\\u0022\\u003EThe free-response section has 4 questions and lasts 1 hour and 40 minutes. This section represents 50% of your total score. Questions 1 and 3 are worth 10 points each. Question 2 is worth 12 points. Question 4 is worth 8 points.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003ETaking the Exam\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp class=\\u0022p1\\u0022\\u003EEach section is timed separately. You can go back and forth between questions in a section until time expires. The clock will turn red when 5 minutes remain in the section\\u2014\\u003Cem\\u003Ethe proctor will not give you any time updates or warnings.\\u003C\\/em\\u003E You cannot move ahead to a different section. Once time expires in a section, you cannot return to it.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp class=\\u0022p1\\u0022\\u003EA calculator and reference information, including lists of equations, are available in this application and can be accessed throughout the exam.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp class=\\u0022p1\\u0022\\u003EYou may use the available paper for scratch work, but credit for Section I: Multiple Choice will only be given for responses entered in this application. When time expires in a section, all answers you have already entered will be submitted.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp class=\\u0022p1\\u0022\\u003EFor Section II: Free Response, the questions are in this application, but you must write your responses to the questions in the free-response booklet. Use a pencil or pen with black or dark blue ink. You may use the available paper for scratch work, but credit will only be given for responses that are written in the free-response booklet.\\u003