\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EThe multiple-choice section has 80 questions and lasts 1 hour. This section represents 50% of your total score. Your score on the multiple-choice section is based on the number of questions answered correctly. Points are not deducted for incorrect answers or unanswered questions.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003ETaking the Exam\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EYou can go back and forth between questions in the section until time expires. The clock will turn red when 5 minutes remain\\u2014\\u003Cem\\u003Ethe proctor will not give you any time updates or warnings\\u003C\\/em\\u003E. Once time expires in the section, you cannot return to it.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EYou may use scratch paper for notes and planning, but credit will only be given for responses entered in this application. Any text you enter as an annotation will \\u003Cem\\u003Enot \\u003C\\/em\\u003Ebe included in your answer submissions. When time expires in the section, all answers you have already entered will be submitted.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EThe inclusion of source material in this exam is not intended as an endorsement by College Board of the content, ideas, or values expressed in the material. The works of art that appear in the AP Art History Exam represent a range of different artists, cultures, techniques, materials, and historical periods, as appropriate for measuring the college-level knowledge and skills that are the focus of this course. AP students are not expected or asked to subscribe to any specific cultural or political values, but they are expected to analyze perspectives that may be different from their own and to interpret works of art in historical context.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EThere may be references in AP testing materials to companies, products, and brands that are owned by others and subject to trademark law. Neither College Board nor the AP testing materials are affiliated with or endorsed by these trademark owners.\\u0