ons.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003ESection II: Free Response\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EThe free-response section has 2 questions and lasts 1 hour and 10 minutes. This section represents 33.3% of your total score. The questions are equally weighted.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003ETaking the Exam\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EEach section is timed separately. You can go back and forth between questions in a section until time expires. The clock will turn red when 5 minutes remain in the section\\u2014\\u003Cem\\u003Ethe proctor will not give you any time updates or warnings\\u003C\\/em\\u003E. You cannot move ahead to a different section. Once time expires in a section, you cannot return to it.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EYou may use scratch paper for notes and planning, but credit will only be given for responses entered in this application. Any text you enter as an annotation will \\u003Cem\\u003Enot\\u003C\\/em\\u003E be included in your answer submissions. When time expires in a section, all answers you have already entered will be submitted.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EThe inclusion of source material in this exam is not intended as an endorsement by College Board of the content, ideas, or values expressed in the material. The sources that appear in the AP Psychology Exam represent a range of data and text-based works, as appropriate for measuring the college-level knowledge and skills that are the focus of this course. AP students are not expected or asked to subscribe to any specific cultural or political values, but they are expected to analyze perspectives that may be different from their own.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EThere may be references in AP testing materials to companies, products, and brands that are owned by others and subject to trademark law. Neither College Board nor the AP testing materials are affiliated with or endorsed by these trademark owners.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\"}],\"cancel_interaction\":[],\"confirm_interaction\":[],\"offering\":\"AP\",\"delivery_method\":\"\",\"changed\":\"\\u003Ctime datetime=\\u00222026-06-25T17:08:14-04:00\\u0022\\u003E1782421694\\u003C\\/time\\u003E\\n\"},{\"id\":\"2576\",\"internal_id\":\"overview-ap-af-am-linear-multiday-part2\",\"type\":\"functional_content\",\"title\":\"Day 2 Exam Overview\",\"short_title\":\"\",\"alternate_title\":\"\",\"internal_description\":\"\",\"external_description\":\"\",\"overview\":\"\",\"content\":[{\"content\":\"\\u003Cp class=\\u0022p1\\u0022\\u003EDay 2 of the AP African American Studies Exam has 2 parts.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003EPart A: Multiple Choice\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp class=\\u0022p1\\u0022\\u003EThe multiple-choice part has 60 questions and lasts 1 hour and 10 minutes. This part represents 60% of your total score. Your score on the multiple-choice part is based on the number of questions answered correctly. Points are not deducted for incorrect answers or unanswered questions.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003EPart B: Short Answer\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp class=\\u0022p1\\u0022\\u003EThe short-answer part includes 1 question about a source for your course project. This part lasts 10 minutes and represents 1.5% of your total score.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003ETaking the Exam\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp class=\\u0022p1\\u0022\\u003EEach part is timed separately. You can go back and forth between questions in a part until time expires. The clock will turn red when 5 minutes remain in a part\\u2014\\u003Cem\\u003Ethe proctor will not give you any time updates or warnings\\u003C\\/em\\u003E. You cannot move ahead to a different part. Once time expires in a part, you cannot return to it.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp class=\\u0022p1\\u0022\\u003EYou may use scratch paper for notes and planning, but credit will only be given for responses entered in this application. Any text you enter as an annotation will \\u003Cem\\u003Enot\\u003C\\/em\\u003E be included in your answer submissions. When time expires in a part, all answers you have already entered will be submitted.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp class=\\u0022p1\\u0022\\u003ESource materials may have been edited for the purpose of this exam.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp class=\\u0022p1\\u0022\\u003EThe inclusion of source material in this exam is not intended as an endorsement by College Board of the content, ideas, or values expressed in the material. The sources that appear in the AP African American Studies Exam represent a range of different works of art, literary texts, historical texts and images, and scholarly articles, as appropriate for measuring the college-level knowledge and skills that are the focus of this course. AP students are not expected or asked to subscribe to any specific cultural or political values, but they are expected to analyze perspectives that may be different from their own and to analyze and interpret sources in their historical and disciplinary contexts.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp class=\\u0022p1\\u0022\\u003EThere may be references in AP testing materials to companies, products, and brands that are owned by others and subject to trademark law. Neither College Board nor the AP testing materials are affiliated with or endorsed by these trademark owners.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\"}],\"cancel_interaction\":[],\"confirm_interaction\":[],\"offering\":\"AP\",\"delivery_method\":\"\",\"changed\":\"\\u003Ctime datetime=\\u00222026-06-25T17:08:14-04:00\\u0022\\u003E1782421694\\u003C\\/time\\u003E\\n\"},{\"id\":\"2551\",\"internal_id\":\"overview-ap-af-am-linear-multiday-part1\",\"type\":\"functional_content\",\"title\":\"Day 1 Exam Overview\",\"short_title\":\"\",\"alternate_title\":\"\",\"internal_description\":\"\",\"external_description\":\"\",\"overview\":\"\",\"content\":[{\"content\":\"\\u003Cp class=\\u0022p1\\u0022\\u003EDay 1 of the AP African American Studies Exam consists of the free-response section.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003EFree Response\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp class=\\u0022p1\\u0022\\u003EThe free-response section has 3 short-answer questions and 1 document-based question. The section lasts 1 hour and 25 minutes. The short-answer questions represent 18% of your total score, and the document-based question represents 12% of your total score.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003ETaking the Exam\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp class=\\u0022p1\\u0022\\u003EYou can go back and forth between questions in the section until time expires. The clock will turn red when 5 minutes remain\\u2014\\u003Cem\\u003Ethe proctor will not give you any time updates or warnings\\u003C\\/em\\u003E. Once time expires in the section, you cannot return to it.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp class=\\u0022p1\\u0022\\u003EYou may use scratch paper for notes and planning, but credit will only be given for responses entered in this application. Any text you enter as an annotation will \\u003Cem\\u003Enot \\u003C\\/em\\u003Ebe included in your answer submissions. When time expires in the section, all answers you have already entered will be submitted.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp class=\\u0022p1\\u0022\\u003ESource materials may have been edited for the purpose of this exam.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp class=\\u0022p1\\u0022\\u003EThe inclusion of source material in this exam is not intended as an endorsement by College Board of the content, ideas, or values expressed in the material. The sources that appear in the AP African American Studies Exam represent a range of different works of art, literary texts, historical texts and images, and scholarly articles, as appropriate for measuring the college-level knowledge and skills that are the focus of this course. AP students are not expected or asked to subscribe to any specific cultural or political values, but they are expected to analyze perspectives that may be different from their own and to analyze and interpret sources in their historical and disciplinary contexts.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp class=\\u0022p1\\u0022\\u003EThere may be references in AP testing materials to companies, products, and brands that are owned by others and subject to trademark law. Neither College Board nor the AP testing materials are affiliated with or endorsed by these trademark owners.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\"}],\"cancel_interaction\":[],\"confirm_interaction\":[],\"offering\":\"AP\",\"delivery_method\":\"\",\"changed\":\"\\u003Ctime datetime=\\u00222026-06-25T17:08:14-04:00\\u0022\\u003E1782421694\\u003C\\/time\\u003E\\n\"},{\"id\":\"2611\",\"internal_id\":\"overview-ap-af-am-linear-singleday\",\"type\":\"functional_content\",\"title\":\"Exam Overview\",\"short_title\":\"\",\"alternate_title\":\"\",\"internal_description\":\"\",\"external_description\":\"\",\"overview\":\"\",\"content\":[{\"content\":\"\\u003Cp class=\\u0022p1\\u0022\\u003EThe AP African American Studies Exam has 2 sections and lasts 2 hours and 45 minutes.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003ESection I, Part A: Multiple Choice\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp class=\\u0022p1\\u0022\\u003EThe multiple-choice part has 60 questions and lasts 1 hour and 10 minutes. This part represents 60% of your total score. Your score on the multiple-choice part is based on the number of questions answered correctly. Points are not deducted for incorrect answers or unanswered questions.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003ESection I, Part B: Short Answer\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp class=\\u0022p1\\u0022\\u003EThe short-answer part includes 1 question about a source for your course project. This part lasts 10 minutes and represents 1.5% of your total score.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003ESection II: Free Response\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp class=\\u0022p1\\u0022\\u003EThe free-response section has 3 short-answer questions and 1 document-based question. The section lasts 1 hour and 25 minutes. The short-answer questions represent 18% of your total score, and the document-based question represents 12% of your total score.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003ETaking the Exam\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp class=\\u0022p1\\u0022\\u003EEach section and part is timed separately. You can go back and forth between questions in a section or part until time expires. The clock will turn red when 5 minutes remain in a section or part\\u2014\\u003Cem\\u003Ethe proctor will not give you any time updates or warnings\\u003C\\/em\\u003E. You cannot move ahead to a different section or part. Once time expires in a section or part, you cannot return to it.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp class=\\u0022p1\\u0022\\u003EYou may use scratch paper for notes and planning, but credit will only be given for responses entered in this application. Any text you enter as an annotation will \\u003Cem\\u003Enot \\u003C\\/em\\u003Ebe included in your answer submissions. When time expires in a section or part, all answers you have already entered will be submitted.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp class=\\u0022p1\\u0022\\u003ESource materials may have been edited for the purpose of this exam.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp class=\\u0022p1\\u0022\\u003EThe inclusion of source material in this exam is not intended as an endorsement by College Board of the content, ideas, or values expressed in the material. The sources that appear in the AP African American Studies Exam represent a range of different works of art, literary texts, historical texts and images, and scholarly articles, as appropriate for measuring the college-level knowledge and skills that are the focus of this course. AP students are not expected or asked to subscribe to any specific cultural or political values, but they are expected to analyze perspectives that may be different from their own and to analyze and interpret sources in their historical and disciplinary contexts.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp class=\\u0022p1\\u0022\\u003EThere may be references in AP testing materials to companies, products, and brands that are owned by others and subject to trademark law. Neither College Board nor the AP testing materials are affiliated with or endorsed by these trademark owners.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\"}],\"cancel_interaction\":[],\"confirm_interaction\":[],\"offering\":\"AP\",\"delivery_method\":\"\",\"changed\":\"\\u003Ctime datetime=\\u00222026-06-25T17:09:00-04:00\\u0022\\u003E1782421740\\u003C\\/time\\u003E\\n\"},{\"id\":\"2631\",\"internal_id\":\"overview-ap-seminar-linear-singleday\",\"type\":\"functional_content\",\"title\":\"Exam Overview\",\"short_title\":\"\",\"alternate_title\":\"\",\"internal_description\":\"\",\"external_description\":\"\",\"overview\":\"\",\"content\":[{\"content\":\"\\u003Cp\\u003EThe AP Seminar Exam consists of 2 parts, Part A and Part B, and lasts 2 hours. There is no break between Part A and Part B. You can move back and forth between Part A and Part B at any time.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003EPart A\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EYou will read a single passage and respond to 3 prompts about that passage. The suggested time for Part A is 30 minutes. You may spend more than 30 minutes if needed, or you may move on to Part B if you finish early.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003EPart B\\u0026nbsp;\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EYou will read 4 sources that present multiple perspectives on the same issue or topic. Before you begin writing your response to Part B, you should carefully read the prompt and the 4 sources. Plan your argument carefully before beginning your response. The suggested time for Part B is 1 hour and 30 minutes.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003ETaking the Exam\\u0026nbsp;\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EPlease note that you are responsible for pacing yourself. The clock will turn red when 5 minutes remain\\u2014\\u003Cem\\u003Ethe proctor will not give you any time updates or warnings\\u003C\\/em\\u003E. You may use scratch paper for notes and planning, but credit will only be given for responses entered in this application. Any text you enter as an annotation will \\u003Cem\\u003Enot \\u003C\\/em\\u003Ebe included in your answer submissions. When time expires, all answers you have already entered will be submitted.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EThe inclusion of source material in this exam is not intended as an endorsement by College Board of the content, ideas, or values expressed in the material. The sources that appear in the AP Seminar Exam explore the complexities of academic and real-world topics and issues by analyzing divergent perspectives, as appropriate for measuring the skills that are the focus of this college-level course. AP students are not expected or asked to subscribe to any specific cultural or political values, but they are expected to analyze perspectives that may be different from their own and to investigate, analyze arguments, compare different perspectives, and synthesize information from multiple sources.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EThere may be references in AP testing materials to companies, products, and brands that are owned by others and subject to trademark law. Neither College Board nor the AP testing materials are affiliated with or endorsed by these trademark owners.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\",\"id\":\"checkin-overview-ap-seminar-p1\"}],\"cancel_interaction\":[],\"confirm_interaction\":[],\"offering\":\"AP\",\"delivery_method\":\"\",\"changed\":\"\\u003Ctime datetime=\\u00222026-06-25T17:09:00-04:00\\u0022\\u003E1782421740\\u003C\\/time\\u003E\\n\"},{\"id\":\"2616\",\"internal_id\":\"overview-ap-comp-sci-p-linear-multiday-part2\",\"type\":\"functional_content\",\"title\":\"Day 2 Exam Overview\",\"short_title\":\"\",\"alternate_title\":\"\",\"internal_description\":\"\",\"external_description\":\"\",\"overview\":\"\",\"content\":[{\"content\":\"\\u003Cp\\u003EDay 2 of the AP Computer Science Principles Exam consists of the multiple-choice section.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003EMultiple Choice\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EThe multiple-choice section has 70 questions and lasts 2 hours. This section represents 70% of your total score. Your score on the multiple-choice section is based on the number of questions answered correctly. Points are not deducted for incorrect answers or unanswered questions.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003ETaking the Exam\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EYou can go back and forth between questions in the section until time expires. The clock will turn red when 5 minutes remain\\u2014\\u003Cem\\u003Ethe proctor will not give you any time updates or warnings\\u003C\\/em\\u003E. Once time expires in the section, you cannot return to it.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EYou may use scratch paper for notes and planning, but credit will only be given for responses entered in this application. Any text you enter as an annotation will \\u003Cem\\u003Enot\\u003C\\/em\\u003E be included in your answer submissions. When time expires in the section, all answers you have already entered will be submitted.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003EReference Information\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EReference information is available by clicking the Reference button in the toolbar. As you encounter programming questions on the multiple-choice section of the exam, you should use the reference information to clarify the behavior of programming statements found in those questions.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EAs AP Computer Science Principles does not designate any particular programming language, these reference materials provide instructions and explanations to help you understand the format and meaning of the questions you will see on the exam. The reference materials include two programming formats: text-based and block-based.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EProgramming instructions in the reference materials use four data types: numbers, Booleans, strings, and lists. Instructions from any of the following categories may appear on the exam:\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cul\\u003E\\u003Cli\\u003EAssignment, Display, and Input\\u003C\\/li\\u003E\\u003Cli\\u003EArithmetic Operators and Numeric Procedures\\u003C\\/li\\u003E\\u003Cli\\u003ERelational and Boolean Operators\\u003C\\/li\\u003E\\u003Cli\\u003ESelection\\u003C\\/li\\u003E\\u003Cli\\u003EIteration\\u003C\\/li\\u003E\\u003Cli\\u003EList Operations\\u003C\\/li\\u003E\\u003Cli\\u003EProcedures and Procedure Calls\\u003C\\/li\\u003E\\u003Cli\\u003ERobot\\u003C\\/li\\u003E\\u003C\\/ul\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EThere may be references in AP testing materials to companies, products, and brands that are owned by others and subject to trademark law. Neither College Board nor the AP testing materials are affiliated with or endorsed by these trademark owners.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\"}],\"cancel_interaction\":[],\"confirm_interaction\":[],\"offering\":\"AP\",\"delivery_method\":\"\",\"changed\":\"\\u003Ctime datetime=\\u00222026-06-25T17:09:00-04:00\\u0022\\u003E1782421740\\u003C\\/time\\u003E\\n\"},{\"id\":\"2596\",\"internal_id\":\"overview-ap-comp-sci-p-linear-multiday-part1\",\"type\":\"functional_content\",\"title\":\"Day 1 Exam Overview\",\"short_title\":\"\",\"alternate_title\":\"\",\"internal_description\":\"\",\"external_description\":\"\",\"overview\":\"\",\"content\":[{\"content\":\"\\u003Cp\\u003EDay 1 of the AP Computer Science Principles Exam consists of the free-response section.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003EFree Response\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EThe free-response section has 2 questions (4 written-response prompts) and lasts 1 hour. This section represents 30% of your total score. The questions for this section are based on the Create Performance Task you previously submitted. Refer to your Personalized Project Reference for the code segments related to your list and procedure. Question 1 represents 5% of your total score, Question 2 represents 15% of your total score, and the Create Performance Task submission\\u2014Program Code, Video, and Personalized Project Reference\\u2014represents 10% of your total score.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003ETaking the Exam\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EYou can go back and forth between questions in the section until time expires. The clock will turn red when 5 minutes remain\\u2014\\u003Cem\\u003Ethe proctor will not give you any time updates or warnings\\u003C\\/em\\u003E. Once time expires in the section, you cannot return to it.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EYou may use scratch paper for notes and planning, but credit will only be given for responses entered in this application. Any text you enter as an annotation will \\u003Cem\\u003Enot\\u003C\\/em\\u003E be included in your answer submissions. When time expires in the section, all answers you have already entered will be submitted.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EThere may be references in AP testing materials to companies, products, and brands that are owned by others and subject to trademark law. Neither College Board nor the AP testing materials are affiliated with or endorsed by these trademark owners.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\"}],\"cancel_interaction\":[],\"confirm_interaction\":[],\"offering\":\"AP\",\"delivery_method\":\"\",\"changed\":\"\\u003Ctime datetime=\\u00222026-06-25T17:09:00-04:00\\u0022\\u003E1782421740\\u003C\\/time\\u003E\\n\"},{\"id\":\"2601\",\"internal_id\":\"overview-ap-comp-sci-p-linear-singleday\",\"type\":\"functional_content\",\"title\":\"Exam Overview\",\"short_title\":\"\",\"alternate_title\":\"\",\"internal_description\":\"\",\"external_description\":\"\",\"overview\":\"\",\"content\":[{\"content\":\"\\u003Cp\\u003EThe AP Computer Science Principles Exam has 2 sections and lasts 3 hours.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003ESection I: Multiple Choice\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EThe multiple-choice section has 70 questions and lasts 2 hours. This section represents 70% of your total score. Your score on the multiple-choice section is based on the number of questions answered correctly. Points are not deducted for incorrect answers or unanswered questions.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003ESection II: Free Response\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EThe free-response section has 2 questions (4 written-response prompts) and lasts 1 hour. This section represents 30% of your total score. The questions for this section are based on the Create Performance Task you previously submitted. Refer to your Personalized Project Reference for the code segments related to your list and procedure. Question 1 represents 5% of your total score, Question 2 represents 15% of your total score, and the Create Performance Task submission\\u2014Program Code, Video, and Personalized Project Reference\\u2014represents 10% of your total score.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003ETaking the Exam\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EEach section is timed separately. You can go back and forth between questions in a section until time expires. The clock will turn red when 5 minutes remain in the section\\u2014\\u003Cem\\u003Ethe proctor will not give you any time updates or warnings\\u003C\\/em\\u003E. You cannot move ahead to a different section. Once time expires in a section, you cannot return to it.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EYou may use scratch paper for notes and planning, but credit will only be given for responses entered in this application. Any text you enter as an annotation will \\u003Cem\\u003Enot\\u003C\\/em\\u003E be included in your answer submissions. When time expires in a section, all answers you have already entered will be submitted.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003EReference Information\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EReference information is available by clicking the \\u003Cstrong\\u003EReference\\u003C\\/strong\\u003E button in the toolbar. As you encounter programming questions on the multiple-choice section of the exam, you should use the reference information to clarify the behavior of programming statements found in those questions.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EAs AP Computer Science Principles does not designate any particular programming language, these reference materials provide instructions and explanations to help you understand the format and meaning of the questions you will see on the exam. The reference materials include two programming formats: text-based and block-based.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EProgramming instructions in the reference materials use four data types: numbers, Booleans, strings, and lists. Instructions from any of the following categories may appear on the exam:\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cul\\u003E\\u003Cli\\u003EAssignment, Display, and Input\\u003C\\/li\\u003E\\u003Cli\\u003EArithmetic Operators and Numeric Procedures\\u003C\\/li\\u003E\\u003Cli\\u003ERelational and Boolean Operators\\u003C\\/li\\u003E\\u003Cli\\u003ESelection\\u003C\\/li\\u003E\\u003Cli\\u003EIteration\\u003C\\/li\\u003E\\u003Cli\\u003EList Operations\\u003C\\/li\\u003E\\u003Cli\\u003EProcedures and Procedure Calls\\u003C\\/li\\u003E\\u003Cli\\u003ERobot\\u003C\\/li\\u003E\\u003C\\/ul\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EThere may be references in AP testing materials to companies, products, and brands that are owned by others and subject to trademark law. Neither College Board nor the AP testing materials are affiliated with or endorsed by these trademark owners.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\",\"id\":\"checkin-overview-ap-comp-sci-p1\"}],\"cancel_interaction\":[],\"confirm_interaction\":[],\"offering\":\"AP\",\"delivery_method\":\"\",\"changed\":\"\\u003Ctime datetime=\\u00222026-06-25T17:09:00-04:00\\u0022\\u003E1782421740\\u003C\\/time\\u003E\\n\"},{\"id\":\"2621\",\"internal_id\":\"overview-ap-eng-lang-linear-multiday-part2\",\"type\":\"functional_content\",\"title\":\"Day 2 Exam Overview\",\"short_title\":\"\",\"alternate_title\":\"\",\"internal_description\":\"\",\"external_description\":\"\",\"overview\":\"\",\"content\":[{\"content\":\"\\u003Cp\\u003EDay 2 of the AP English Language and Composition Exam consists of the multiple-choice section.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003EMultiple Choice\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EThe multiple-choice section has 45 questions and lasts 1 hour. This section represents 45% of your total score. Your score on the multiple-choice section is based on the number of questions answered correctly. Points are not deducted for incorrect answers or unanswered questions.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003ETaking the Exam\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EYou can go back and forth between questions in the section until time expires. The clock will turn red when 5 minutes remain\\u2014\\u003Cem\\u003Ethe proctor will not give you any time updates or warnings\\u003C\\/em\\u003E. Once time expires in the section, you cannot return to it.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EYou may use scratch paper for notes and planning, but credit will only be given for responses entered in this application. Any text you enter as an annotation will \\u003Cem\\u003Enot\\u003C\\/em\\u003E be included in your answer submissions. When time expires in a section, all answers you have already entered will be submitted.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EThe inclusion of source material in this exam is not intended as an endorsement by College Board of the content, ideas, or values expressed in the material. The nonfiction works and excerpts that appear in the AP English Language and Composition Exam represent a range of different authors, styles, and real-world topics, as appropriate for measuring the analytic and argumentative writing and rhetorical analysis skills that are the focus of this college-level course. AP students are not expected or asked to subscribe to any specific cultural or political values, but they are expected to analyze perspectives that may be different from their own and to question the meaning, purpose, or effect of such content within nonfiction works.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EThere may be references in AP testing materials to companies, products, and brands that are owned by others and subject to trademark law. Neither College Board nor the AP testing materials are affiliated with or endorsed by these trademark owners.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\",\"id\":\"checkin-overview-ap-eng-lang-day2-p1\"}],\"cancel_interaction\":[],\"confirm_interaction\":[],\"offering\":\"AP\",\"delivery_method\":\"\",\"changed\":\"\\u003Ctime datetime=\\u00222026-06-25T17:09:00-04:00\\u0022\\u003E1782421740\\u003C\\/time\\u003E\\n\"},{\"id\":\"2626\",\"internal_id\":\"overview-ap-eng-lang-linear-multiday-part1\",\"type\":\"functional_content\",\"title\":\"Day 1 Exam Overview\",\"short_title\":\"\",\"alternate_title\":\"\",\"internal_description\":\"\",\"external_description\":\"\",\"overview\":\"\",\"content\":[{\"content\":\"\\u003Cp\\u003EDay 1 of the AP English Language and Composition Exam consists of the free-response section.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003EFree Response\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EThe free-response section has 3 questions and lasts 2 hours and 15 minutes. This section represents 55% of your total score. Each of the 3 questions counts equally toward your score.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003ETaking the Exam\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EYou can go back and forth between questions in the section until time expires. The clock will turn red when 5 minutes remain\\u2014\\u003Cem\\u003Ethe proctor will not give you any time updates or warnings\\u003C\\/em\\u003E. Once time expires in the section, you cannot return to it.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EYou may use scratch paper for notes and planning, but credit will only be given for responses entered in this application. Any text you enter as an annotation will \\u003Cem\\u003Enot\\u003C\\/em\\u003E be included in your answer submissions. When time expires in a section, all answers you have already entered will be submitted.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EThe inclusion of source material in this exam is not intended as an endorsement by College Board of the content, ideas, or values expressed in the material. The nonfiction works and excerpts that appear in the AP English Language and Composition Exam represent a range of different authors, styles, and real-world to