ection represents 50% of your total score. Your score on the multiple-choice section is based on the number of questions answered correctly. Points are not deducted for incorrect answers or unanswered questions.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003ETaking the Exam\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EYou can go back and forth between questions in the section until time expires. The clock will turn red when 5 minutes remain\\u2014\\u003Cem\\u003Ethe proctor will not give you any time updates or warnings\\u003C\\/em\\u003E. Once time expires in the section, you cannot return to it.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EYou may use scratch paper for notes and planning, but credit will only be given for responses entered in this application. Any text you enter as an annotation will \\u003Cem\\u003Enot\\u003C\\/em\\u003E be included in your answer submissions. When time expires in a section, all answers you have already entered will be submitted.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EThe inclusion of source material in this exam is not intended as an endorsement by College Board of the content, ideas, or values expressed in the material. The sources and excerpts that appear in the AP Latin Exam represent a range of works, as appropriate for measuring the skills that are the focus of this college-level course. AP students are not expected or asked to subscribe to any specific cultural or political values, but they are expected to analyze perspectives that may be different from their own.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EThere may be references in AP testing materials to companies, products, and brands that are owned by others and subject to trademark law. Neither College Board nor the AP testing materials are affiliated with or endorsed by these trademark owners.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\"}],\"cancel_interaction\":[],\"confirm_interaction\":[],\"offering\":\"AP\",\"delivery_method\":\"\",\"changed\":\"\\u003Ctime datetime=\\u00222026-06-25T17:07:28-04:00\\u0022\\u003E1782421648\\u003C\\/time\\u003E\\n\"},{\"id\":\"2531\",\"internal_id\":\"overview-ap-latin-linear-multiday-part1\",\"type\":\"functional_content\",\"title\":\"Day 1 Exam Overview\",\"short_title\":\"\",\"alternate_title\":\"\",\"internal_description\":\"\",\"external_description\":\"\",\"overview\":\"\",\"content\":[{\"content\":\"\\u003Cp\\u003EDay 1 of the AP Latin Exam consists of the free-response section.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003EFree Response\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EThe free-response section includes 5 questions and lasts 1 hour and 55 minutes. This section represents 50% of your total exam score. Questions 1 through 3 each represent 10% of your score. Questions 4 and 5, along with the in-class project checkpoints, represent 20% of your score.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003ETaking the Exam\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EYou can go back and forth between questions in the section until time expires. The clock will turn red when 5 minutes remain\\u2014\\u003Cem\\u003Ethe proctor will not give you any time updates or warnings\\u003C\\/em\\u003E. Once time expires in the section, you cannot return to it.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EYou may use scratch paper for notes and planning, but credit will only be given for responses entered in this application. Any text you enter as an annotation will \\u003Cem\\u003Enot \\u003C\\/em\\u003Ebe included in your answer submissions. When time expires in a section, all answers you have already entered will be submitted.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EThe inclusion of source material in this exam is not intended as an endorsement by College Board of the content, ideas, or values expressed in the material. The sources and excerpts that appear in the AP Latin Exam represent a range of works, as appropriate for measuring the skills that are the focus of this college-level course. AP students are not expected or asked to subscribe to any specific cultural or political values, but they are expected to analyze perspectives that may be different from their own.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EThere may be references in AP testing materials to companies, products, and brands that are owned by others and subject to trademark law. Neither College Board nor the AP testing materials are affiliated with or endorsed by these trademark owners.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\"}],\"cancel_interaction\":[],\"confirm_interaction\":[],\"offering\":\"AP\",\"delivery_method\":\"\",\"changed\":\"\\u003Ctime datetime=\\u00222026-06-25T17:07:28-04:00\\u0022\\u003E1782421648\\u003C\\/time\\u003E\\n\"},{\"id\":\"2521\",\"internal_id\":\"overview-ap-latin-linear-singleday\",\"type\":\"functional_content\",\"title\":\"Exam Overview\",\"short_title\":\"\",\"alternate_title\":\"\",\"internal_description\":\"\",\"external_description\":\"\",\"overview\":\"\",\"content\":[{\"content\":\"\\u003Cp\\u003EThe AP Latin Exam has 2 sections and lasts 3 hours.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003ESection I: Multiple Choice\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EThe multiple-choice section has 52 questions and lasts 1 hour and 5 minutes. This section represents 50% of your total score. Your score on the multiple-choice section is based on the number of questions answered correctly. Points are not deducted for incorrect answers or unanswered questions.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003ESection II: Free Response\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EThe free-response section includes 5 questions and lasts 1 hour and 55 minutes. This section represents 50% of your total exam score. Questions 1 through 3 each represent 10% of your score. Questions 4 and 5, along with the in-class project checkpoints, represent 20% of your score.\\u003C\\/p\\u003E\\u003Ch2\\u003ETaking the Exam\\u003C\\/h2\\u003E\\u003Cp\\u003EEach section is timed separately. You can go back and forth between questions in a section until time expires. The clock will turn red when 5 minutes remain in the section\\u2014\\u003Cem\\u003Ethe proctor will not give you any time updates or warnings\\u003C\\/em\